WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.454
As an opportunity
to stand back and

00:00:07.455 --> 00:00:11.550
ask ourselves what we mean when
we talk about intelligence.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:17.450
And I guess there are a couple
of reasons to think about this.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.630
We operate now, and I call for

00:00:20.631 --> 00:00:24.850
a moment where there are sort
of hyped up versions of what

00:00:24.851 --> 00:00:27.310
we mean when we talk
about intelligence.

00:00:27.311 --> 00:00:30.190
And obviously we're all here
because of one of those versions

00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:34.700
which is, of course,
machine intelligence and AI.

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:38.290
And I think it's incumbent
on us to ask ourselves what

00:00:38.291 --> 00:00:41.580
we're actually dealing with when
we're talking about machines

00:00:41.581 --> 00:00:44.280
that operate in some realm of,

00:00:44.281 --> 00:00:47.350
if you'd like,
artificial intelligence.

00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:51.110
The other probably more
practical reason is, of course,

00:00:51.111 --> 00:00:53.030
if we get the answer
to this wrong,

00:00:53.031 --> 00:00:55.580
people won't use
the things that you build.

00:00:55.581 --> 00:00:57.290
And there are numerous reasons,

00:00:57.291 --> 00:01:00.820
numerous examples that we
see all over the place of

00:01:00.821 --> 00:01:04.460
intelligence being imbedded
in various software and

00:01:04.461 --> 00:01:08.810
hardware devices that we use and
people just switching it off.

00:01:08.811 --> 00:01:12.260
And really that's a problem
that I feel like we face

00:01:12.261 --> 00:01:16.440
in Microsoft and the research
people that I work in.

00:01:16.441 --> 00:01:20.640
It's confronting AI and thinking
when will people actually want

00:01:20.641 --> 00:01:25.610
to use something that introduces
some kind of interpretation.

00:01:25.611 --> 00:01:27.620
And I think that's a genuine
question that we need to

00:01:27.621 --> 00:01:28.520
ask ourselves.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.820
So, Rebecca had
three principles.

00:01:33.821 --> 00:01:36.100
I'm gonna talk about
three stories.

00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:40.540
Three if you like versions if
you will of What I see to be

00:01:40.541 --> 00:01:45.420
intelligence, or what I would go
on to talk about, capability or

00:01:45.421 --> 00:01:47.230
capacity in practice.

00:01:47.231 --> 00:01:52.060
And this idea of capability
is really important to me,

00:01:52.061 --> 00:01:54.930
what I feel like we're dealing
with when we talk about

00:01:54.931 --> 00:02:00.140
intelligence is how
the world makes us capable.

00:02:00.141 --> 00:02:03.410
How things in the world,
how what we do in the world

00:02:03.411 --> 00:02:08.830
enables us to be capable,
capable agents, capable actors.

00:02:08.831 --> 00:02:11.880
And what I hope to talk about
through my three stories is how

00:02:11.881 --> 00:02:14.520
people are being made
capable in practice.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.460
So, my first story is about,
and obviously there

00:02:20.461 --> 00:02:24.210
are different ways in which we
might talk about these examples.

00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:27.110
But I wanna talk about
vision in this case.

00:02:27.111 --> 00:02:29.990
And I'm gonna talk about some

00:02:29.991 --> 00:02:33.740
field work that I've done with
a number of people in my group.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.490
Around interventional radiology.

00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:40.330
And some of you may know
interventional radiology is

00:02:40.331 --> 00:02:44.590
a new form of radiology in
which, thanks to various things

00:02:44.591 --> 00:02:49.730
like keyhole surgery and new
forms of instruments that allow

00:02:49.731 --> 00:02:54.100
cameras to be placed in bodies,
surgeons can now

00:02:54.101 --> 00:02:58.290
combine the scanning of
the body with surgery.

00:02:58.291 --> 00:03:03.499
So prior to this, most of
the scanning would happen

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:09.310
pre-surgery and surgeons would
look through the imagery and

00:03:09.311 --> 00:03:11.390
then operate on the body.

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:15.210
What you'll see in this video,
I think it's a two minute video,

00:03:15.211 --> 00:03:21.350
or three minute video, is a
surgeon directing a radiologist

00:03:21.351 --> 00:03:25.264
to continually update an x-ray.

00:03:25.265 --> 00:03:28.890
And later on, I don't think
it's in this video, they go and

00:03:28.891 --> 00:03:34.090
look at these MRI scans that are
on the LCD panels in the back

00:03:34.091 --> 00:03:39.270
of the room, what I want you
to pay attention to is how

00:03:39.271 --> 00:03:43.860
the surgeon is getting to
see the body, if you will.

00:03:43.861 --> 00:03:46.816
How vision is, if you will,
is enacted in practice and

00:03:46.817 --> 00:03:49.452
I'll talk through this in
a bit more detail, but

00:03:49.453 --> 00:03:50.823
let's watch the video.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.884
[INAUDIBLE]

00:03:59.086 --> 00:04:02.585
[INAUDIBLE]

00:04:26.423 --> 00:04:36.423
[INAUDIBLE]

00:04:42.992 --> 00:04:46.674
in

00:04:46.675 --> 00:04:56.674
here.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.990
The kinds of things
that computer vision

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.910
might introduce in recognizing
scans of the body.

00:05:06.911 --> 00:05:10.690
What is that the way in
which a surgeon starts to

00:05:10.691 --> 00:05:14.385
understand [INAUDIBLE]
That he's involved in,

00:05:14.386 --> 00:05:20.045
a collaboration with other
people in the surgery.

00:05:20.046 --> 00:05:22.645
It's a very tactile thing.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.400
Of inserting a cable
into our body, and

00:05:32.401 --> 00:05:35.110
feeling it not with responding
the way he's expected.

00:05:35.111 --> 00:05:38.420
So in this case what
the assistant's doing is,

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:43.440
he's doing an operation to fuse
two vertebrae together that

00:05:43.441 --> 00:05:46.680
have been an injury
the patient's been subjected to.

00:05:46.681 --> 00:05:51.480
And he's inserting a wire into
one of the vertebrae to create

00:05:51.481 --> 00:05:55.800
a thread that he will
then tap some screws.

00:05:55.801 --> 00:05:58.460
So then you can join
these two vertebrae

00:05:58.461 --> 00:06:00.530
together through some screws.

00:06:00.531 --> 00:06:04.060
But what he's finding is again
through this tactile sensation

00:06:04.061 --> 00:06:08.550
of the wire not gripping into
the burn The burn is in fact is

00:06:08.551 --> 00:06:11.610
soft, softer that he
would have expected.

00:06:11.611 --> 00:06:16.380
And what they go onto do is
said to these pre ops scans and

00:06:16.381 --> 00:06:17.520
look at the and

00:06:17.521 --> 00:06:21.980
think, is there some suggestion
that the bone might be soft?

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:24.090
And it turns out that it is and
in fact,

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.460
unfortunately the patient
has a cancer But

00:06:29.461 --> 00:06:32.300
it's only through those
activities, through the sequence

00:06:32.301 --> 00:06:35.710
of activities and the sensation
of the wire in the bone,

00:06:35.711 --> 00:06:39.210
through the collaboration
with these people in surgery,

00:06:39.211 --> 00:06:41.810
through looking again at
the sequence of X-rays and

00:06:41.811 --> 00:06:45.440
then at the pre-op scans that,
that discovery was made.

00:06:45.441 --> 00:06:49.500
It wasn't made by looking at
the pre-ops scans by themselves.

00:06:49.501 --> 00:06:52.650
And this is what I mean about
being made capable as it were.

00:06:52.651 --> 00:06:55.680
The surgeon here is
being made capable

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:57.750
through this interactional
sequence of events.

00:06:57.751 --> 00:07:02.870
Let me bring up that

00:07:02.871 --> 00:07:08.960
video again Let me turn
to my second story.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:13.960
And again there are many things
to kind of ponder on here in

00:07:13.961 --> 00:07:17.120
this example but I wanted to
sort of thing about how speech

00:07:17.121 --> 00:07:19.790
is being used because obviously
that's one of the things that we

00:07:19.791 --> 00:07:22.190
think about with machine
learning, machine intelligence.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:28.660
This is a video taken
from a workshop that two

00:07:28.661 --> 00:07:33.570
of us attended in which we're
observing some people preparing

00:07:33.571 --> 00:07:37.730
to teach blind children
various sports activities.

00:07:37.731 --> 00:07:39.520
And in this case,

00:07:39.521 --> 00:07:42.310
they're practicing an activity
that they'll do with the blind

00:07:42.311 --> 00:07:44.280
children of throwing and
catching.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.360
It's important to know that
Warren here on the right,

00:07:49.361 --> 00:07:53.640
has no central vision, and
very limited peripheral vision.

00:07:53.641 --> 00:07:56.730
And James is his guide, and

00:07:56.731 --> 00:07:59.480
they're gonna have a go
at throwing and catching.

00:07:59.481 --> 00:08:01.700
I should also say
that off screen,

00:08:01.701 --> 00:08:04.590
there's another couple
throwing and catching,

00:08:04.591 --> 00:08:08.550
and That's the noise that's
coming from outside of screen.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.444
&gt;&gt; So what we're going to do
is we're going to find out how

00:08:14.445 --> 00:08:17.440
to send And
we're going to look at different

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:23.440
ways that we can modify things
to make it appropriate for

00:08:23.441 --> 00:08:26.380
the people that in your group
&gt;&gt; So first of all,

00:08:26.381 --> 00:08:29.285
sending simply means making
the ball go away from you.

00:08:29.286 --> 00:08:33.290
And receiving simply
means the ball in again.

00:08:33.291 --> 00:08:36.000
So that could be a throw and
a catch or

00:08:36.001 --> 00:08:37.970
it could be a kick and a stop.

00:08:37.971 --> 00:08:39.640
It's up to you entirely.

00:08:39.641 --> 00:08:42.700
So will you just get with your
partner and work out how you're

00:08:42.701 --> 00:08:45.211
going to send and receive this
ball to each other [LAUGH]

00:08:45.212 --> 00:08:46.144
&gt;&gt; Do you mind?

00:08:46.145 --> 00:08:49.890
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; I can't [INAUDIBLE], but

00:08:49.891 --> 00:08:50.720
all right.

00:08:50.721 --> 00:08:52.009
&gt;&gt; I like [INAUDIBLE] week.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:53.376
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I've cut back in me.

00:08:53.377 --> 00:08:55.208
I like [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:55.209 --> 00:08:56.900
It's good, isn't it?

00:08:56.901 --> 00:08:57.980
&gt;&gt; You do that.

00:08:57.981 --> 00:08:58.780
Do that.

00:09:01.058 --> 00:09:02.130
Try again.

00:09:02.131 --> 00:09:03.380
&gt;&gt; I'm gonna try.

00:09:03.381 --> 00:09:04.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sometimes here we go.

00:09:04.631 --> 00:09:07.021
You should have that.

00:09:07.022 --> 00:09:09.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Try again, try again.

00:09:09.461 --> 00:09:12.455
&gt;&gt; So great about-
&gt;&gt; Just to say that-

00:09:12.456 --> 00:09:14.204
&gt;&gt; Beating him.

00:09:14.205 --> 00:09:16.473
&gt;&gt; They-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.850
&gt;&gt; Go on to do it continuously.

00:09:21.851 --> 00:09:25.850
&gt;&gt; To the point where James
eventually drops the kick and

00:09:25.851 --> 00:09:27.330
Warren catches it.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:32.290
So what we might imagine to
be something that a blind

00:09:32.291 --> 00:09:35.210
person would find incredibly
hard to accomplish turns

00:09:35.211 --> 00:09:39.290
out to be something that they
do from the second try onwards.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:44.630
Now, I've asked you to think
about the speech that's

00:09:44.631 --> 00:09:45.170
going on there.

00:09:45.171 --> 00:09:47.770
And I'm not sure, I mean I've
watched this video again and

00:09:47.771 --> 00:09:51.010
again, this is sort of one of
the things I do as a sociologist

00:09:51.011 --> 00:09:54.290
is really pay attention to
the interactional sequences that

00:09:54.291 --> 00:09:56.270
occur in events like this.

00:09:56.271 --> 00:09:58.910
So I'm not sure if you'll
have noticed it but,

00:09:58.911 --> 00:10:01.300
what's happening while they're
throwing and catching.

00:10:01.301 --> 00:10:03.120
And as they begin to throw and
catch.

00:10:03.121 --> 00:10:05.920
And, again,
you may observe this as,

00:10:05.921 --> 00:10:08.000
they don't just start
throwing and catching.

00:10:08.001 --> 00:10:09.460
They just hand the ball
to each other.

00:10:09.461 --> 00:10:11.620
They tap the ball
in preparation,

00:10:11.621 --> 00:10:14.200
hand it to the next person.

00:10:14.201 --> 00:10:17.000
Warren hands it to James,
James taps it,

00:10:17.001 --> 00:10:17.930
hands it back to Warren.

00:10:17.931 --> 00:10:20.500
So there's this building up
of a sequence of actions,

00:10:20.501 --> 00:10:24.580
almost in preparation for
the throwing and catching.

00:10:24.581 --> 00:10:25.920
And the talk happens

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.210
in synchrony with that
passing the ball.

00:10:30.211 --> 00:10:33.440
And so it's as though the talk
is preparing the thrower and

00:10:33.441 --> 00:10:37.260
the catcher for what will
then be eventually the throw.

00:10:37.261 --> 00:10:37.840
And the catch.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:40.920
I don't think that's
a coincidence.

00:10:40.921 --> 00:10:44.030
I think a lot of work
is being done here

00:10:44.031 --> 00:10:47.150
interactionally to
help Warren see.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:51.080
Seeing here is not
a purely visual or

00:10:51.081 --> 00:10:54.034
physiological activity,
it's an interactional one.

00:10:54.035 --> 00:10:58.066
Seeing that Warren does
something accomplished through

00:10:58.067 --> 00:11:01.930
the tap on the ball, through
the passing of the ball and

00:11:01.931 --> 00:11:04.282
the sequence of interactions and

00:11:04.283 --> 00:11:08.500
through language being sat
on top of those activities.

00:11:08.501 --> 00:11:10.950
So when we talk about
natural language processing

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:14.900
it's problematic to
extricate it if you will

00:11:14.901 --> 00:11:16.870
from the activities
that are unfolding.

00:11:16.871 --> 00:11:21.650
And this is an area of
machine learning that I'm

00:11:21.651 --> 00:11:24.870
deeply involved now is sort of
working with people in natural

00:11:24.871 --> 00:11:28.460
language processing to think
more carefully about how we,

00:11:28.461 --> 00:11:32.680
if you like ground on the data
that we have of talk.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:38.240
So the last story that
I want to work through

00:11:38.241 --> 00:11:40.290
is this is a slightly
longer video.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:46.680
It's a video from a while back
in which we recorded people in

00:11:46.681 --> 00:11:50.720
cars, just to better
understand how

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:54.820
domestic life extends beyond
the boundaries of the home.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.600
Well, actually,

00:11:57.601 --> 00:12:01.200
I'm just gonna play the video
cuz it sorta speaks for itself.

00:12:01.201 --> 00:12:02.580
It's, I think, a three or

